{
  "organisms": [
    {
      "id": "staph_aureus",
      "name": "Staphylococcus aureus",
      "group": "Gram-positive",
      "morphology": "Cocci in clusters",
      "tagline": "Catalase- and coagulase-positive pathogen behind skin infection, bacteraemia, endocarditis and toxin-mediated disease.",
      "diseases": [
        "Skin and soft tissue infection (abscess, cellulitis, impetigo)",
        "Bacteraemia and infective endocarditis",
        "Osteomyelitis and septic arthritis",
        "Pneumonia (post-influenza, necrotising)",
        "Toxic shock syndrome",
        "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome",
        "Food poisoning (preformed enterotoxin)"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-positive cocci in clusters; facultative anaerobe, non-motile, non-spore-forming.\n• Catalase-positive and coagulase-positive (separates it from coagulase-negative staphylococci); ferments mannitol; beta-haemolytic on blood agar.\n• MRSA carries mecA (or mecC), encoding PBP2a, which has low affinity for beta-lactams."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Protein A binds the Fc region of IgG and impairs opsonisation.\n• Coagulase and clumping factor promote clot formation and adherence.\n• Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL) is linked to necrotising skin and lung infection.\n• Superantigens (TSST-1, enterotoxins) cause toxic shock and food poisoning; exfoliative toxins cause scalded skin syndrome.\n• Biofilm formation on prosthetic material and catheters."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "Roughly 20-30% of people carry S. aureus in the anterior nares. Spread is mainly by direct contact, often from the patient's own colonised sites. Risk factors include injection drug use, indwelling devices, dialysis, diabetes, surgery and recent hospitalisation. Community-associated MRSA is a frequent cause of skin abscesses."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Blood cultures (at least 2 sets) for suspected bacteraemia; repeat every 24-48 h until clear.\n• Pus or abscess fluid for Gram stain and culture.\n• Rapid PCR (mecA/nuc) identifies MRSA quickly.\n• S. aureus in blood is never a contaminant: search for the source and consider echocardiography."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• MSSA bacteraemia: an anti-staphylococcal beta-lactam (cefazolin, or flucloxacillin, nafcillin or oxacillin) is first-line. Vancomycin is inferior for MSSA.\n• MRSA bacteraemia: vancomycin (AUC-guided dosing) or daptomycin.\n• Uncomplicated bacteraemia: at least 14 days from the first negative culture. Endocarditis, osteomyelitis and metastatic foci need 4-6 weeks or longer.\n• Source control (drain abscesses, remove infected devices) is essential.\n• Mild MRSA skin infection: incision and drainage; add TMP-SMX or doxycycline when antibiotics are indicated."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "Hand hygiene, contact precautions for MRSA, device-care bundles, and decolonisation (intranasal mupirocin with chlorhexidine bathing) in selected patients before surgery."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Skin: painful abscess or boil, impetigo, folliculitis, cellulitis.\n• Bacteraemia with metastatic seeding to bone, joints, lungs and heart valves.\n• Right-sided endocarditis in injection drug users; left-sided disease destroys valves quickly.\n• Enterotoxin food poisoning: vomiting within 1 to 6 hours of eating, resolves in a day.\n• Toxic shock: fever, rash, hypotension and multi-organ involvement.\n• Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome in infants and young children."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• mecA (or mecC) encodes PBP2a, giving resistance to nearly all beta-lactams except newer anti-MRSA cephalosporins.\n• Penicillinase (blaZ) makes most isolates penicillin-resistant.\n• VISA from a thickened cell wall; VRSA via vanA acquired from enterococci (rare).\n• Inducible clindamycin resistance (erm): detect with the D-test."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "S. aureus bacteraemia carries high mortality; early source control and infectious diseases input improve outcomes.",
        "For MSSA, beta-lactams outperform vancomycin; do not default to vancomycin once susceptibility is known.",
        "Document clearance with repeat blood cultures."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Liu C et al. IDSA clinical practice guidelines for MRSA infections in adults and children. Clin Infect Dis 2011;52:e18-e55.",
        "Tong SYC et al. Staphylococcus aureus infections: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical manifestations, and management. Clin Microbiol Rev 2015;28:603-661.",
        "Holland TL et al. Clinical management of Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia: a review. JAMA 2014;312:1330-1341."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "strep_pneumoniae",
      "name": "Streptococcus pneumoniae",
      "group": "Gram-positive",
      "morphology": "Lancet-shaped diplococci",
      "tagline": "Encapsulated, alpha-haemolytic pathogen; leading cause of community-acquired pneumonia and adult bacterial meningitis.",
      "diseases": [
        "Community-acquired pneumonia (lobar)",
        "Bacterial meningitis",
        "Acute otitis media and sinusitis",
        "Bacteraemia and sepsis",
        "Overwhelming post-splenectomy infection"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci; alpha-haemolytic on blood agar.\n• Catalase-negative, optochin-sensitive and bile-soluble.\n• Polysaccharide capsule (more than 100 serotypes) is the main virulence factor; the Quellung reaction shows capsule swelling."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Capsule inhibits phagocytosis.\n• Pneumolysin damages host cells and impairs immune function.\n• IgA protease aids mucosal colonisation; autolysin (LytA) releases inflammatory cell-wall fragments.\n• Surface proteins (PspA, choline-binding proteins) aid adhesion and complement evasion."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "Colonises the nasopharynx, especially in children, and spreads by respiratory droplets. Highest risk: children under 2 years, adults 65 and older, asplenia or hyposplenism, sickle cell disease, immunodeficiency, chronic heart, lung, liver or kidney disease, cochlear implants and CSF leak. Conjugate vaccines have shifted the circulating serotypes."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Blood cultures and sputum Gram stain and culture.\n• Urinary pneumococcal antigen supports the diagnosis of pneumonia in adults.\n• CSF Gram stain, culture and PCR when meningitis is suspected.\n• Penicillin susceptibility breakpoints differ for meningitis and non-meningitis isolates."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Outpatient CAP in healthy adults: amoxicillin or doxycycline; a macrolide only where local pneumococcal macrolide resistance is below 25%.\n• Hospitalised CAP (non-severe): beta-lactam plus macrolide, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone alone.\n• Meningitis: ceftriaxone plus vancomycin empirically, narrowed to susceptibility; give dexamethasone with or before the first antibiotic dose in adults.\n• Macrolide resistance is widespread, so macrolide monotherapy is unreliable."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "Pneumococcal conjugate and polysaccharide vaccines per national schedules. Vaccinate patients with asplenia, and before splenectomy or cochlear implantation."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Lobar pneumonia: abrupt fever and rigors, productive cough with rust-coloured sputum, pleuritic chest pain, lobar consolidation.\n• Meningitis: the leading cause of adult bacterial meningitis; headache, neck stiffness and altered mental state, with a risk of hearing loss.\n• Otitis media and sinusitis, especially in children.\n• Bacteraemia and sepsis; overwhelming post-splenectomy infection in asplenia and sickle cell disease.\n• Complications: parapneumonic effusion, empyema, pericarditis.\n• Risk groups: extremes of age, asplenia, alcohol use disorder, COPD, HIV, CSF leak, immunoglobulin or complement deficiency."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Penicillin resistance results from altered penicillin-binding proteins, not beta-lactamase.\n• Susceptibility breakpoints differ for meningitis and non-meningitis infection; pneumonia is often still treatable with high-dose penicillin or amoxicillin.\n• Macrolide resistance by erm (target modification) and mef (efflux) is common, so avoid macrolide monotherapy where local rates are high.\n• Fluoroquinolone resistance through gyrA and parC mutations is uncommon.\n• Empirical meningitis therapy includes vancomycin plus a third-generation cephalosporin because of resistant strains.\n• Pneumococcal conjugate vaccines have shifted circulating serotypes (serotype replacement)."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Alpha-haemolytic, optochin-sensitive, bile-soluble: the classic laboratory triad.",
        "Most common cause of both community-acquired pneumonia and adult bacterial meningitis.",
        "Asplenic patients can progress to fulminant sepsis within hours; treat fever as an emergency."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Metlay JP et al. Diagnosis and treatment of adults with community-acquired pneumonia. ATS/IDSA guideline. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2019;200:e45-e67.",
        "van de Beek D et al. ESCMID guideline: diagnosis and treatment of acute bacterial meningitis. Clin Microbiol Infect 2016;22(Suppl 3):S37-S62.",
        "Weiser JN, Ferreira DM, Paton JC. Streptococcus pneumoniae: transmission, colonization and invasion. Nat Rev Microbiol 2018;16:355-367."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "e_coli",
      "name": "Escherichia coli",
      "group": "Gram-negative",
      "morphology": "Bacilli (rods)",
      "tagline": "Gut commensal and the most common cause of urinary tract infection and Gram-negative bacteraemia.",
      "diseases": [
        "Urinary tract infection (cystitis, pyelonephritis)",
        "Sepsis and bacteraemia",
        "Intra-abdominal infection",
        "Neonatal meningitis (K1 capsule)",
        "Traveller's diarrhoea (ETEC)",
        "Haemorrhagic colitis and haemolytic uraemic syndrome (STEC)"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-negative bacilli, facultative anaerobe, motile.\n• Lactose fermenter: pink colonies on MacConkey agar, green metallic sheen on EMB agar.\n• Indole-positive and oxidase-negative.\n• Pathotypes: ExPEC (UPEC, neonatal meningitis), ETEC, EPEC, EHEC/STEC, EIEC and EAEC."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• P fimbriae and type 1 fimbriae mediate urinary tract adhesion.\n• K1 capsule is linked to neonatal meningitis.\n• LPS endotoxin drives septic shock.\n• Shiga toxin (EHEC O157:H7) causes haemorrhagic colitis and HUS; heat-labile and heat-stable toxins (ETEC) cause watery diarrhoea.\n• Siderophores for iron acquisition."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "Part of normal gut flora. Diarrhoeal pathotypes spread by the faecal-oral route through contaminated food or water. ESBL-producing and carbapenem-resistant strains are rising worldwide, particularly in healthcare settings."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Urine culture (100,000 CFU/mL is the classic threshold; lower counts can be significant in symptomatic patients).\n• Blood cultures for sepsis.\n• Test stool for Shiga toxin or stx genes when STEC is suspected; do not rely on O157 culture alone.\n• Request ESBL and carbapenemase testing per local policy."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Uncomplicated cystitis: nitrofurantoin, fosfomycin or TMP-SMX where local resistance is low; avoid fluoroquinolones as first-line.\n• Pyelonephritis and bacteraemia: ceftriaxone or a fluoroquinolone guided by susceptibility; step down to oral therapy once improving.\n• ESBL-producing isolates: a carbapenem for serious infection outside the urinary tract; oral TMP-SMX, ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin step-down is accepted when susceptible.\n• Carbapenem-resistant isolates: treatment depends on the carbapenemase type; follow IDSA AMR guidance.\n• STEC (EHEC): supportive care; avoid antibiotics and antimotility agents because of the risk of HUS."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "Food and water hygiene, hand hygiene, minimising and promptly removing urinary catheters, and antimicrobial stewardship."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Cystitis: dysuria, frequency, suprapubic pain; pyelonephritis adds fever and flank pain.\n• ETEC: watery traveller's diarrhoea without fever; EHEC: bloody diarrhoea with little or no fever.\n• HUS (haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, acute kidney injury) follows EHEC, mostly in children.\n• Neonatal meningitis and sepsis in the first weeks of life.\n• Biliary and intra-abdominal sepsis from gut translocation."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• ESBLs (mainly CTX-M) and plasmid AmpC.\n• Carbapenemases: KPC, NDM, OXA-48.\n• Widespread fluoroquinolone and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole resistance.\n• Resistance genes spread on plasmids between Enterobacterales."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Pink on MacConkey (lactose fermenter) and indole-positive: E. coli.",
        "Avoid antibiotics in EHEC O157:H7 diarrhoea; HUS can follow haemorrhagic colitis, especially in children.",
        "Do not treat asymptomatic bacteriuria, except in pregnancy and before urological procedures with mucosal trauma."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Kaper JB, Nataro JP, Mobley HLT. Pathogenic Escherichia coli. Nat Rev Microbiol 2004;2:123-140.",
        "Gupta K et al. IDSA/ESCMID guideline for acute uncomplicated cystitis and pyelonephritis in women. Clin Infect Dis 2011;52:e103-e120.",
        "Tamma PD et al. IDSA guidance on the treatment of antimicrobial-resistant Gram-negative infections. Clin Infect Dis 2024."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pseudomonas",
      "name": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa",
      "group": "Gram-negative",
      "morphology": "Non-fermenting bacilli",
      "tagline": "Opportunistic, intrinsically resistant pathogen of ventilated, neutropenic, burn and cystic fibrosis patients.",
      "diseases": [
        "Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia",
        "Bacteraemia in neutropenia (ecthyma gangrenosum)",
        "Burn and wound infection",
        "Chronic lung infection in cystic fibrosis",
        "Otitis externa and malignant otitis externa",
        "Catheter-associated urinary tract infection",
        "Keratitis in contact-lens wearers"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-negative, aerobic, non-lactose-fermenting bacillus; oxidase-positive and motile (polar flagellum).\n• Pyocyanin (blue-green) and pyoverdine (yellow-green) pigments; grape-like odour.\n• Grows at 42 C, which helps to distinguish it from other pseudomonads."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Exotoxin A inhibits elongation factor 2 (the same target as diphtheria toxin).\n• Type III secretion system injects ExoS, ExoT, ExoU and ExoY.\n• Alginate biofilm (mucoid phenotype in cystic fibrosis) and quorum sensing.\n• Elastase, phospholipase C and pyocyanin cause tissue damage."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "Found in moist environments such as sinks, water systems and respiratory equipment. Risk factors: neutropenia, mechanical ventilation, burns, cystic fibrosis, indwelling devices and prolonged hospitalisation. Intrinsic resistance (low outer-membrane permeability, efflux pumps, chromosomal AmpC) is compounded by acquired resistance, including loss of OprD and carbapenemases."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Culture of the relevant site, with susceptibility testing on every isolate.\n• Difficult-to-treat resistance (DTR) means non-susceptibility to piperacillin-tazobactam, ceftazidime, cefepime, aztreonam, meropenem, imipenem-cilastatin, ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• An anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam is the backbone: piperacillin-tazobactam, cefepime, ceftazidime or meropenem, chosen by susceptibility.\n• Combination therapy is not routinely needed once susceptibility is known.\n• Ciprofloxacin is the main oral option.\n• DTR isolates: ceftolozane-tazobactam, ceftazidime-avibactam or imipenem-cilastatin-relebactam are preferred; cefiderocol is an alternative.\n• Inhaled tobramycin is used for chronic cystic fibrosis infection."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "Hand hygiene, device-care bundles, control of water and environmental sources, and contact precautions for resistant strains."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia.\n• Neutropenic bacteraemia with ecthyma gangrenosum: painless black necrotic eschar with surrounding erythema.\n• Burn wound infection: blue-green discharge with a sweet, grape-like odour.\n• Cystic fibrosis: chronic lung infection with mucoid, alginate-producing strains.\n• Otitis externa (swimmer's ear) and malignant otitis externa in older diabetic patients, with skull base osteomyelitis and cranial nerve palsy.\n• Hot-tub folliculitis; catheter-associated urinary infection; keratitis in contact-lens wearers.\n• Osteomyelitis after foot puncture wounds through footwear; endocarditis in injection drug users."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Intrinsically resistant to ampicillin, amoxicillin-clavulanate, first- and second-generation cephalosporins, ceftriaxone, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and tetracyclines (low outer-membrane permeability, efflux pumps, chromosomal AmpC).\n• Acquired: efflux up-regulation (MexAB-OprM), loss of the OprD porin (carbapenem resistance), AmpC derepression during therapy, ESBLs, carbapenemases (VIM, IMP, NDM, KPC).\n• Aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes and gyrA mutations cause aminoglycoside and fluoroquinolone resistance.\n• Resistance can emerge during treatment, so repeat cultures if the patient is not improving.\n• Difficult-to-treat resistance: newer agents such as ceftolozane-tazobactam, ceftazidime-avibactam, imipenem-relebactam or cefiderocol, guided by susceptibility testing.\n• Biofilms in cystic fibrosis and device infections make eradication hard."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Oxidase-positive, non-lactose fermenter with blue-green pigment and a grape-like odour.",
        "Think Pseudomonas in neutropenic fever, ventilator-associated pneumonia, burns, hot-tub folliculitis and ecthyma gangrenosum.",
        "Ceftriaxone and ertapenem have no reliable anti-pseudomonal activity."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Tamma PD et al. IDSA guidance on the treatment of antimicrobial-resistant Gram-negative infections. Clin Infect Dis 2024.",
        "Qin S et al. Pseudomonas aeruginosa: pathogenesis, virulence factors, antibiotic resistance, interaction with host, technology advances and emerging therapeutics. Signal Transduct Target Ther 2022;7:199."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "m_tuberculosis",
      "name": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis",
      "group": "Acid-fast",
      "morphology": "Slender acid-fast bacilli",
      "tagline": "Slow-growing intracellular pathogen causing granulomatous disease; a leading infectious cause of death worldwide.",
      "diseases": [
        "Pulmonary tuberculosis",
        "Miliary tuberculosis",
        "Tuberculous meningitis",
        "Spinal tuberculosis (Pott disease)",
        "Tuberculous lymphadenitis (scrofula)",
        "Genitourinary and pericardial tuberculosis",
        "Latent tuberculosis infection"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Obligate aerobe, non-motile, non-spore-forming bacillus with a lipid-rich cell wall containing mycolic acids.\n• Acid-fast on Ziehl-Neelsen or Kinyoun stain; auramine-rhodamine fluorescence is used for screening.\n• Very slow growth (doubling about 15-20 h): Lowenstein-Jensen solid medium takes 3-8 weeks; liquid culture (MGIT) is faster.\n• Niacin-positive and nitrate reductase-positive."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Cord factor (trehalose dimycolate) and mycolic acids protect against killing and promote granuloma formation.\n• Sulfatides inhibit phagosome-lysosome fusion.\n• ESX-1 secretion system (ESAT-6, CFP-10) supports intracellular survival and tissue damage.\n• The cell-mediated (type IV) hypersensitivity response drives caseous necrosis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "Spread by airborne droplet nuclei. An estimated quarter of the world's population has been infected. Progression to disease is most likely with HIV, immunosuppression (anti-TNF therapy, corticosteroids), diabetes, malnutrition, silicosis, chronic kidney disease and young age."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Syndromes",
          "text": "• Primary TB: Ghon complex, often asymptomatic.\n• Reactivation TB: apical cavitary disease with cough, haemoptysis, night sweats, fever and weight loss.\n• Extrapulmonary: lymph nodes, pleura, bone and spine, meninges, genitourinary tract, pericardium.\n• Miliary TB: haematogenous spread with a millet-seed pattern on chest X-ray."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Sputum acid-fast smear and culture, with a rapid molecular test (Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra) that also detects rifampicin resistance.\n• Chest radiograph: apical infiltrates and cavitation.\n• Latent infection: IGRA or tuberculin skin test; neither separates latent from active disease.\n• Always obtain drug-susceptibility testing."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Drug-susceptible pulmonary TB: 2 months of isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol (HRZE), then 4 months of isoniazid and rifampicin.\n• Give pyridoxine (vitamin B6) with isoniazid and monitor liver function.\n• TB meningitis and bone TB need longer courses; follow current national guidance. Corticosteroids are indicated in TB meningitis and TB pericarditis.\n• A 4-month rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen is an option for eligible patients with drug-susceptible disease.\n• MDR/RR-TB: all-oral regimens such as BPaLM (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin) for 6 months per WHO guidance.\n• Latent TB: short rifamycin-based regimens (3HP, 4R or 3HR) per local guidance."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "BCG vaccine (protects mainly against severe childhood TB), airborne isolation of infectious patients, contact tracing and treatment of latent infection in high-risk groups."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• MDR-TB is resistance to at least isoniazid and rifampin; rifampin-resistant TB is treated like MDR-TB; XDR-TB adds fluoroquinolone and bedaquiline or linezolid resistance.\n• Main mutations: rpoB (rifampin), katG and inhA (isoniazid), embB (ethambutol), pncA (pyrazinamide), gyrA (fluoroquinolones).\n• Causes: incomplete or non-adherent treatment, inadequate regimens, and direct transmission of resistant strains.\n• Detection: Xpert MTB/RIF identifies rpoB mutations; line probe assays, whole genome sequencing and phenotypic susceptibility testing confirm the pattern.\n• Treatment: WHO now recommends shorter all-oral regimens, such as BPaLM (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin), under specialist supervision.\n• Never add a single drug to a failing regimen; use directly observed therapy to protect adherence."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Rifampicin: orange body fluids and many drug interactions. Isoniazid: neuropathy and hepatitis. Pyrazinamide: hyperuricaemia. Ethambutol: optic neuritis.",
        "A negative smear does not exclude TB; use culture or molecular testing.",
        "Test every person with TB for HIV."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Nahid P et al. Official ATS/CDC/ERS/IDSA clinical practice guidelines: treatment of drug-susceptible tuberculosis. Clin Infect Dis 2016;63:e147-e195.",
        "Pai M et al. Tuberculosis. Nat Rev Dis Primers 2016;2:16076.",
        "WHO. Consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis: treatment. Geneva: World Health Organization."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s_pyogenes",
      "name": "Streptococcus pyogenes",
      "group": "Gram-positive",
      "morphology": "Gram-positive cocci in chains (Group A Streptococcus)",
      "tagline": "Beta-haemolytic Group A Streptococcus causing pharyngitis, skin infection and invasive disease, with post-infectious rheumatic fever and glomerulonephritis.",
      "diseases": [
        "Pharyngitis and scarlet fever",
        "Impetigo and erysipelas",
        "Cellulitis",
        "Necrotizing fasciitis",
        "Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome",
        "Acute rheumatic fever",
        "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-positive cocci in chains; catalase-negative.\n• Beta-haemolytic on blood agar (complete clearing), Lancefield group A.\n• Bacitracin-sensitive and PYR-positive, which separates it from other beta-haemolytic streptococci."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• M protein: antiphagocytic, the basis of serotyping, and linked to rheumatic fever through molecular mimicry.\n• Hyaluronic acid capsule resembles host tissue and is poorly immunogenic.\n• Streptolysin O (oxygen-labile, drives the ASO response) and streptolysin S.\n• Pyrogenic exotoxins SpeA, SpeB and SpeC act as superantigens (scarlet fever, toxic shock).\n• Streptokinase, DNases and hyaluronidase promote spread through tissue.\n• C5a peptidase inactivates complement chemotaxis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Humans are the only reservoir; spread by respiratory droplets and direct contact with skin lesions.\n• Pharyngitis peaks in school-age children (5 to 15 years) in winter and spring; impetigo is more common in warm, humid and low-resource settings.\n• Rheumatic heart disease remains a major cause of cardiac disease in low- and middle-income countries.\n• Invasive disease is more frequent in older adults, diabetes, injection drug use, the postpartum period and after varicella."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Pharyngitis: sudden sore throat, fever, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior cervical nodes, no cough.\n• Scarlet fever: sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue, circumoral pallor.\n• Erysipelas: raised, sharply demarcated red plaque; cellulitis is deeper and less defined.\n• Necrotizing fasciitis: pain out of proportion to the skin findings, rapid spread, shock.\n• Rheumatic fever follows pharyngitis only, not skin infection; glomerulonephritis can follow throat or skin infection."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Rapid antigen detection test; a negative test in children should be backed up by throat culture.\n• Throat culture on blood agar remains the reference method.\n• Anti-streptolysin O and anti-DNase B document recent infection; anti-DNase B is better after skin infection.\n• Necrotizing fasciitis is a clinical and surgical diagnosis: do not wait for imaging."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Penicillin V or amoxicillin remains the drug of choice; penicillin resistance has never been documented.\n• Non-severe penicillin allergy: cephalexin. Severe allergy: clindamycin or a macrolide (resistance varies locally).\n• Necrotizing fasciitis and toxic shock: urgent surgical debridement, penicillin plus clindamycin to suppress toxin production, and consider IVIG.\n• Treatment of pharyngitis within 9 days of onset prevents rheumatic fever but does not prevent glomerulonephritis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Macrolide and clindamycin resistance are rising in many regions.\n• Tetracycline resistance is common, so doxycycline is not reliable.\n• Penicillin and beta-lactam susceptibility remains universal."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Secondary prophylaxis with long-acting benzathine penicillin after rheumatic fever.\n• Close contact prophylaxis is considered in selected invasive cases.\n• No licensed vaccine."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Rheumatic fever follows throat infection; glomerulonephritis can follow throat or skin.",
        "Pain out of proportion on a red limb is necrotizing fasciitis until proven otherwise.",
        "Clindamycin is added in severe disease because it works on toxin synthesis, not only on killing."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
