      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Curved Gram-negative bacilli, highly motile with a single polar flagellum.\n• Oxidase-positive; ferments glucose and sucrose, so it makes yellow colonies on TCBS agar.\n• Tolerates alkaline conditions (alkaline peptone water enrichment) and is killed by gastric acid.\n• O1 (classical and El Tor biotypes) and O139 serogroups cause epidemic cholera."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Cholera toxin (encoded by the CTX phage) is an AB5 toxin: the A subunit ADP-ribosylates Gs, raising cAMP and driving chloride and water secretion.\n• Toxin-coregulated pilus (TCP) mediates colonization and is the receptor for CTX phage.\n• Mucinase, neuraminidase and flagellar motility help penetrate the mucus layer.\n• Non-invasive: the organism stays in the lumen, so there is no blood or pus in the stool."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Transmitted through contaminated water and food (including raw shellfish); outbreaks follow floods, conflict and displacement.\n• Endemic in South Asia, sub-Saharan Africa and parts of the Middle East; the seventh pandemic (El Tor) began in 1961.\n• Major outbreaks: Haiti (2010 onwards) and Yemen (2016 onwards).\n• Estimated 1.3 to 4 million cases and 21,000 to 143,000 deaths per year worldwide.\n• Most infections are mild or asymptomatic, but they can still spread the organism."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Incubation from a few hours to 5 days.\n• Sudden painless watery diarrhoea, 'rice-water' stool, with vomiting; fluid loss can reach 1 litre per hour.\n• Dehydration: sunken eyes, absent skin turgor, thirst, oliguria, hypotension and shock.\n• Hypokalaemia (cramps, arrhythmia) and metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate loss.\n• Without treatment, death can occur within hours; with prompt rehydration, mortality is under 1%."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Clinical diagnosis in an outbreak setting; start rehydration immediately.\n• Stool or rectal swab culture on TCBS agar; dark-field microscopy shows darting motility.\n• Rapid dipstick tests for O1 and O139 antigen are useful for field surveillance.\n• PCR for the ctxA gene confirms toxigenic strains.\n• Report suspected cases to public health authorities."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Rehydration is the lifesaving treatment: oral rehydration solution for mild and moderate dehydration, and intravenous Ringer's lactate for severe dehydration.\n• Monitor fluid balance and replace ongoing losses; correct potassium and acidosis.\n• Antibiotics shorten illness and reduce stool volume in moderate to severe cases: single-dose azithromycin (or doxycycline in non-pregnant adults) is commonly used; follow local resistance patterns.\n• Zinc supplementation for children.\n• Continue feeding, and breastfeeding in infants."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Tetracycline resistance and multidrug-resistant strains carrying the SXT integrating conjugative element (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, streptomycin, chloramphenicol) are reported in many regions.\n• Reduced ciprofloxacin susceptibility is common in El Tor strains.\n• Azithromycin remains active in most areas, but check local surveillance."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Safe drinking water, sanitation and handwashing.\n• Oral cholera vaccines (killed, two doses, or a single-dose live vaccine for travellers) are used in endemic areas, outbreaks and for travellers at risk.\n• Thorough cooking of seafood and food safety.\n• Household and community measures during outbreaks, plus targeted chemoprophylaxis for close contacts where recommended."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
